    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Extreme weather events often have consequences not only economically and financially,\r\nbut also in terms of more complex social changes. How people respond to\r\ndisasters in their risk-taking, prosociality, and other social behaviors dependent on\r\ngroup membership is stilla a somewhat open question. This paper explores changes\r\nin charitable giving among New York area neighborhoods with significant exposure\r\nto flood damages from Hurricane Sandy after the storm. While the existence of any\r\nmajor shift in giving levels is unlikely, there is the possibility of a differential effect\r\nbetween Republicans and Democrats, with the former increasing contributions while\r\nthe latter see no change. Additionally, I provide further evidence to suggest a strong\r\nprice discount on single family homes in the Sandy flood zone following the hurricane,\r\nbut do not find evidence of changes in rents.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Semenza, A. (2022). Charitable Giving and the Housing Market after Hurricane Sandy in New York City. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/sx379dr8858",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
